WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
There's a company quietly holding the world's&nbsp;
software hostage, and no one is talking about it.&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.600
Broadcom, the eighth largest company&nbsp;
on earth, paid $69 billion for VMware.&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.960
You’ve probably never heard of it, but globally,&nbsp;
banks, grocery stores, telecom networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.040
government agencies, all rely on it.
But after Broadcom bought it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.400
despite promising no price increases,&nbsp;
customers saw costs jump as much as 1000%.&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.480
They’re now fighting lawsuits from&nbsp;
the likes of AT&amp;T, T-Mobile, Tesco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.440
while also fighting regulators, with one&nbsp;
fining Broadcom a quarter million euros a day.&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.040
Broadcom is even sending cease and&nbsp;
desists to their own customers.&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.960
The regulators who were supposed&nbsp;
to stop this — the FTC, the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.720
the European Commission — every&nbsp;
single one of them let it through.&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.600
Broadcom is holding the world’s software hostage,&nbsp;
and you and I may end up paying the price…&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.640
To find out how this happened, we need&nbsp;
to go back before the acquisition.&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.080
Because you might be thinking “How on&nbsp;
earth did regulators let this one through?”&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.600
A crucial question, that we’ll get to soon.
But first, what the heck is VMware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.640
and why does it matter?
VMware just means “Virtual Machine-ware”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.680
You might have heard that term, “virtual machine”.
It basically means a fake computer running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.600
inside a real one.
This is how people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.680
run Windows, even while on a Mac.
Sounds niche, but there’s a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.080
chance you interact with VMware every&nbsp;
single day, just without knowing it.&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.840
It was created in 1998, with the purpose of&nbsp;
running virtual machines on Intel’s x86 hardware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:47.200
and it solved a problem, which changed everything.
Before it, you could realistically only run one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.600
“application” per physical server, because&nbsp;
if you ran multiple… and one crashes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.960
they all crash. It just wasn’t reliable.
So you had lots of small individual workloads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:03.360
and servers, which only ran at about 10% capacity.
Because they’re separate, if use on one spikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.440
it can’t draw from another which isn’t used.
Virtual machines solved this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.200
and this changed the world.
You can have one big server running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.680
lots and lots of fake computers.
And if one crashes, it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.440
take the others down with it.
VMware meant more efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.600
but also meant fewer servers, less cooling,&nbsp;
energy consumption usually drops by 80%.&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.360
This is quite simplified, and VMware&nbsp;
offers a lot of different software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:36.320
products… but this affects, basically everything.
Online banking, grocery stores, telecom services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:42.400
major government infrastructure like tunnels&nbsp;
and bridges, VMware is huge, and everywhere.&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.480
VMware had become a near monopoly. It held&nbsp;
about 70% of the virtualization market.&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.040
But all this brings us back to Broadcom,&nbsp;
the 8th largest company on earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.280
worth about 1.7 trillion dollars. Larger&nbsp;
than Meta, Tesla, and Saudi Aramco.&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.560
And the villain of our story: President Hock Tan.
Tan has a bit of a history here.&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.480
In 2018 he tried to buy Qualcomm, before he was&nbsp;
blocked by the Trump Administration, thankfully.&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:16.720
But soon enough, his eyes wandered to VMware,&nbsp;
and Tan saw a huge area of untapped revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.640
Unfortunately, it would make everyone else's&nbsp;
lives much worse, and Broadcom way more money.&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:29.680
But to the shock of many, the US, UK, and most&nbsp;
importantly, the EU all approved the acquisition.&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:36.080
In November 2023, Broadcom acquired VMware&nbsp;
for $61 billion, or at $69 billion including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.600
VMware’s debt.
What? How?&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.120
How on earth did the Broadcom&nbsp;
takeover get approved?&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.080
And what did Tan have planned, that would end&nbsp;
with lawsuits, mass migration away from VMware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.680
and Broadcom sending cease and&nbsp;
desists… to existing customers?&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:04:05.440
Great question, because… I’m&nbsp;
still a bit confused myself.&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.200
Speaking of software, how many times have&nbsp;
you tried to learn a new coding language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.840
or coding in general, only to quit?
Ironically, in the age of vibe coding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.560
knowing code is more important than ever,&nbsp;
and gives you a big advantage in any career.&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.720
People across every industry are now&nbsp;
building their own apps and products.&nbsp;

