00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.920
The FTC, the Justice Department, the UK CMA, and&nbsp;
the European Commission, all made a fatal mistake…&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.400
They were all concerned with the risks&nbsp;
to the hardware market… not software.&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.800
The EU focused on fibre channel adapters,
the UK focused on server hardware components,&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.400
and the US raised a similar hardware issue, but&nbsp;
ultimately chose not to block the acquisition.&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.720
The risks here weren’t high enough to block&nbsp;
the deal… but none of them thought about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.560
would harm customers the most:
VMware’s licence structure.&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.360
In a blog in 2022 about VMware, Hock Tan wrote:
[Hock Tan, Broadcom President]: “The Broadcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.080
business case for this transaction is premised&nbsp;
on focusing the business model, increasing R&amp;D,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.960
and executing in getting customers to use the&nbsp;
full portfolio of innovative product offerings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.680
not on increasing prices.”
Sounds good right? But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.480
this is actually way worse. And notice&nbsp;
the “not on increasing prices“ part.&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.920
He also said “I’ve continued to&nbsp;
see questions in press reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.840
about whether we intend to raise prices on&nbsp;
VMware products. The answer is simple: No.”&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.480
Because this seems to contradict what followed.
After the acquisition, customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.560
costs went up across the board.
Managed service providers serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:44.480
doctors’ offices and small retailers saw a 10x&nbsp;
increase in cost, from around $50-70K per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.800
to a minimum of $650K+.
A lot of businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:55.360
saw price jumps of 300 to 400%.
European cloud providers saw cost hikes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:02.880
800% to 1,500%! Eight to fifteen times higher!
Tesco, the giant UK grocery chain, said some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:10.960
VMware costs jumped around 175 percent, while&nbsp;
other VMware costs jumped 350%! Which it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.680
described as “manifestly unfair and excessive.”&nbsp;
They even had an existing contract from 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.800
which Broadcom basically ignored.
But one of the biggest was AT&amp;T&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.920
“After a 10 plus year strategic relationship with&nbsp;
Broadcom … I am sad to report that we appear to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:35.040
at an impasse on our VMware deal, This proposed&nbsp;
annual increase of +1,050 percent in one year is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.800
extreme and certainly not how we expect strategic&nbsp;
partners to engage in doing business with AT&amp;T.”&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.160
What followed, was outrage, lawsuits,&nbsp;
complaints to regulators, and much more.&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.320
But… Hock Tan promised he&nbsp;
wouldn’t increase prices…&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.480
Well, Broadcom didn’t technically.&nbsp;
They did something much worse.&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:08:00.720
Broadcom made two colossal changes:
They stopped selling perpetual VMware licences.&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.400
Previously customers bought&nbsp;
VMware once, and owned it forever.&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.680
And those customers still generated money,&nbsp;
because they paid for ongoing support from VMware.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:15.760
Updates, security patches, support, etc.
But Broadcom, after the acquisition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.240
stopped providing paid security patches&nbsp;
and support… to existing perpetual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:25.360
owners. Not that they wouldn’t update the&nbsp;
software, you couldn’t pay for it, at all.&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.400
For important infrastructure like VMware,&nbsp;
you can’t just not update it either.&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.760
Security needs to be continuously updated and&nbsp;
maintained, especially in cases like banks or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.760
the government, where gaps in security&nbsp;
could have catastrophic consequences.&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:45.120
After the acquisition, Broadcom notified AT&amp;T&nbsp;
that support for its roughly 75,000 virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:52.480
machines running across 8,600 servers would end.
So how did existing customers… still use VMware?&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.480
That was the final part of the trap.
Broadcom was pushing perpetual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.720
customers… to a new subscription.
But not just any subscription.&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.080
Broadcom reduced the VMware product catalogue&nbsp;
from over 70 product families, to just 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:11.200
and gave customers just 30 days of notice.
This meant hundreds of standard products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.000
people used, like individual SKUs,&nbsp;
great for small businesses, were gone.&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.240
You had to upgrade to the entire bundle,&nbsp;
either the VMware Cloud Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:26.880
or the VMware vSphere Foundation. “All new&nbsp;
support must be via VCF or VVF subscription.”&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.800
That’s why all those companies, including&nbsp;
gigantic conglomerates, technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:38.400
had price hikes as high as 1000% or more.
This, is diabolical, but we’re not even done.&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.040
Broadcom sent cease and desists to their&nbsp;
own perpetual customers, against downloading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.440
security patches.
Yeah.&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.960
But, you can’t just up and leave VMware.
It’s not software you can just change or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.840
uninstall, for a lot of industries,&nbsp;
virtualization is very foundational.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:02.720
It’s described as “bone-breaking engineering”.
Migrating is slow and expensive… and risky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.200
Exactly what Broadcom was counting on.
Perfect for someone who wants to upcharge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:13.360
customers, as… they can’t really go&nbsp;
anywhere else. At least not quickly.&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:19.600
And what happened next, was all the ways it&nbsp;
backfired, some Broadcom didn’t anticipate.&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:32.960
But there might still be ways you and&nbsp;
I pay the price for this acquisition.&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:36.480
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.960
means you won’t miss future videos.
Hock Tan bragged that Broadcom got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.800
more than 90% of VMware’s 10,000 largest&nbsp;
customers re-signed to new contracts.&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:53.280
But some of the actual largest, certainly&nbsp;
haven’t, and are fighting Broadcom at every turn.&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.120
First are the lawsuits.
AT&amp;T sued Broadcom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:02.640
as it owned perpetual licences, and had a contract&nbsp;
that allowed it to extend support even further.&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:09.520
Broadcom, allegedly, went back on that contract.
This was especially important, because AT&amp;T&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.560
had a 25 year contract for FirstNet. The&nbsp;
federal wireless first-responder network.&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:20.000
AT&amp;T in the lawsuit said “Broadcom’s&nbsp;
attempt to bully AT&amp;T into paying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.880
king’s ransom for subscriptions AT&amp;T does&nbsp;
not want or need, or risk widespread network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.360
outages that could cripple the operations&nbsp;
of millions of AT&amp;T customers worldwide”.&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:35.360
And Broadcom’s response was… weird.
“If AT&amp;T truly felt the support services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:40.320
were essential to avoid irreparable harm to its&nbsp;
customers, it would have exercised and purchased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.760
the ‘three-year-renewal’ option”
Odd.&nbsp;

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:48.640
The two eventually settled out of court,&nbsp;
but this is far from the only lawsuit.&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.480
T-Mobile also sued Broadcom, to&nbsp;
try to force Broadcom to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.360
providing support for perpetual licences.
"In its complaint, T-Mobile said it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:04.800
tens of thousands of virtual machines using VMware&nbsp;
software across approximately 303,140 CPU cores.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:10.080
It also said that it was migrating off VMware but&nbsp;
noted the time-consuming and technical challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:16.880
involved in migrating over 1,000 applications."
Tesco is also suing Broadcom for over £100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:23.040
million, and accused them of “abusive conduct”.
The grocery giant bought perpetual VMware licences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:29.440
in 2021, with support through 2026, plus&nbsp;
the option to extend it another four years.&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:35.120
But, Tesco says Broadcom refused to continue&nbsp;
standalone support, which they already paid for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:41.680
and demanded they move to subscription&nbsp;
bundles, and pushed prices up ~175%.&nbsp;

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:45.280
But it gets even worse.
The Dutch Ministry of Infrastructure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:51.360
Water Management, which has used VMware for over&nbsp;
15 years, suddenly saw an 85 percent jump in cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:56.560
to use the same software. From around&nbsp;
$2.4 million to nearly $4.6 million.&nbsp;

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:01.600
It asked Broadcom for a support extension&nbsp;
of two to three years while it migrated out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.520
which Broadcom refused, unless&nbsp;
it moved to a new platform.&nbsp;

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:09.120
This is a vital ministry which runs&nbsp;
the Netherlands’ tunnels, locks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.800
and bridges, essentially held hostage by Broadcom.&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:17.040
That was, until a Dutch Court found&nbsp;
Broadcom had breached its duty of care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:23.600
and ordered two years more support, at&nbsp;
reasonable cost, or be fined €250,000… per day.&nbsp;

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:30.320
Gotta say, that verdict feels pretty good to see.
This has also caused an EU trade body, CISPE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:36.240
to look much closer at Broadcom. CISPE sued,&nbsp;
not Broadcom, but the European Commission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:42.080
to void the original merger approval, arguing&nbsp;
regulators didn't properly scrutinise the deal.&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:47.440
Which, in fairness, is true. They didn’t.
Broadcom tried to fight back, bidding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.520
stop regulators requesting US&nbsp;
legal documents tied to VMware.&nbsp;

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.960
But the bid was denied, letting&nbsp;
the investigation continue.&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.240
There’s no doubt VMware is&nbsp;
making much more profit now.&nbsp;

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:05.840
VMware's operating margins went from 13–22%&nbsp;
pre-acquisition to 77% under Broadcom.&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:09.520
But long term, this may have&nbsp;
backfired spectacularly.&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:16.320
T-Mobile, Western Union, GEICO, Computershare,&nbsp;
Danfoss, are all moving away from VMware.&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:21.520
Regardless of the lawsuit outcome, Tesco has&nbsp;
also started migrating 40,000 servers away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:26.640
from VMware. “The decision to migrate servers&nbsp;
while simultaneously suing the vendor reflects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:31.680
how badly the relationship has broken down.”
“The VMware exodus is creating a market for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:37.360
alternatives that did not exist two years ago.”
And it’s certainly helping competitors.&nbsp;

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:43.840
Competitor Nutanix’s CEO Rajiv Ramaswami described&nbsp;
the pace of Broadcom defections as “steady and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:48.000
climbing”, and could continue for five years.
“I think there's no doubt that the customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.960
sentiment continues to be&nbsp;
negative about Broadcom,”&nbsp;

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:58.400
A survey of over 110 VMware customers in late&nbsp;
2024 found 98% are using, planning, or considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:04.080
alternatives to at least part of their VMware&nbsp;
estate, and 92% expect further price hikes.&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:10.960
CloudBolt estimated VMware’s virtualization market&nbsp;
share could drop from 70% to 40%, in just 5 years.&nbsp;

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:17.360
But, even so, for all the companies that just&nbsp;
decide to pay up, the cost might pass on to us.&nbsp;

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:21.600
Even if prices of everything don’t&nbsp;
immediately jump, companies often don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:28.400
just “absorb” new costs without consequence.
Cloud providers absorb some, then slowly pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:34.240
on the rest to their own customers. Maybe not you&nbsp;
or me, but their own business customers, who buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:39.840
cloud hosting, who now have a higher IT cost.
Eventually, the end B2C product might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.880
go up in price, or down in quality.
Or if governments have to pay Broadcom more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:51.120
for virtualization, that's more tax dollars, more&nbsp;
of your money, which could go to other services.&nbsp;

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.440
As a report by ECCO, an industry group&nbsp;
representing European cloud companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:01.360
stated “[VMware] price hikes have been absorbed&nbsp;
internally (at the expense of innovation) and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:06.240
externally (at the expense of consumers).”
Broadcom took something people owned and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:10.480
turned it into rent, but there’s another&nbsp;
company that set the trend for all this:&nbsp;

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.280
Adobe.&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:16.560
Turning owned software into a subscription, and&nbsp;
keeping customers trapped with excessive fees.&nbsp;

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:21.440
Now, they might just be getting what they deserve.
